        * name : text
          description : text
          config_variant : text
          configuration_id : uuid <<FK>>
    }

    note right of ores_dq_todays_market_configs_artefact_tbl
    One ORE todaysmarket.xml document, held as the thing the collections and the configurations hang off. The document itself carries no market data: it is an ordered set of collections, each entry naming a curve by reference, plus named configurations that select one collection of each kind by the collection's id. The entries live on todays_market_entry, the configurations on todays_market_configuration, and the references a configuration makes on todays_market_configuration_binding. This row is only the identity of the document, so that a tenant may hold more than one and a report may choose between them. - Artefact Table
    end note

    entity ores_dq_todays_market_configuration_bindings_artefact_tbl  #F7E5FF {
        --
        * dataset_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * tenant_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * id : uuid
        * version : integer
        * party_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * todays_market_configuration_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * collection : text
        * reference : text
        * position : integer
    }

    note right of ores_dq_todays_market_configuration_bindings_artefact_tbl
    One reference a configuration makes. A Configuration block writes up to twenty-four of these. Each names a collection kind and the id of one collection of that kind: DiscountingCurvesId inccy selects the DiscountingCurves collection whose id is inccy. The reference is text, not a foreign key to todays_market_collection. ORE resolves it by name when it builds the market, and the export only has to write it back. Whether it should become a real key is left to the work that first resolves it, which is producing the engine input from the entities. - Artefact Table
    end note

    entity ores_dq_todays_market_configurations_artefact_tbl  #F7E5FF {
        --
        * dataset_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * tenant_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * id : uuid
        * version : integer
        * party_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * todays_market_config_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * configuration_id : text <<FK>>
        * position : integer
    }

    note right of ores_dq_todays_market_configurations_artefact_tbl
    One Configuration block. It is not a collection of entries: it is a bundle of references, each naming one entry of one collection, so that a report can select a coherent set of curves and volatilities by name instead of restating them. It identifies itself by a required id attribute, which is unique within a document. The references it makes live on todays_market_configuration_binding. - Artefact Table
    end note

    entity ores_dq_todays_market_entries_artefact_tbl  #F7E5FF {
        --
        * dataset_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * tenant_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * id : uuid
        * version : integer
        * party_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * todays_market_config_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * todays_market_collection_id : uuid <<FK>>
          key_value : text
          key_value_2 : text
        * target : text
          discounting : text
        * position : integer
    }

    note right of ores_dq_todays_market_entries_artefact_tbl
    One entry of one collection. Twenty-three of the document's twenty-four collections are this shape: an entry identifying itself by one attribute and carrying a reference as its text, in a position. The attribute is not one name. It is name for nineteen collections, currency for DiscountingCurves, pair for the two FX collections and the literal string key for SwaptionVolatilities and CapFloorVolatilities. Which one is a fact about the collection's kind, so it is held once on todays_market_collection_kind rather than repeated on every entry. SwaptionVolatilities and CapFloorVolatilities identify by two attributes at once, so key_value_2 carries the second. SwapIndexCurves is the only collection whose entry nests, and discounting carries its one child. - Artefact Table
    end note

    entity ores_dq_trading_account_types_artefact_tbl  #F7E5FF {
        --
        * dataset_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * tenant_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * code : text
        * version : integer
          source : text
          description : text
        * coding_scheme_code : text <<FK>>
    }

    note right of ores_dq_trading_account_types_artefact_tbl
    Classifies a trading account by whose activity it carries: a client's, the firm's own, or pooled client positions. The rows arrive from the FpML account type codelist through DQ. Named for the trading account because the IAM component owns the type of a login account. Each row is one value of an FpML coding scheme: a code, unique within its coding scheme, with the source that defines it and a description. The rows arrive through DQ from the FpML codelists. - Artefact Table
    end note

    entity ores_dq_yield_curve_configs_artefact_tbl  #F7E5FF {
        --
        * dataset_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * tenant_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * id : uuid
        * version : integer
        * party_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * curve_definition_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * currency : text
        * discount_curve : text
          interpolation_variable : text
          interpolation_method : text
          mixed_interpolation_cutoff : integer
          day_counter : text
          tolerance : double
          extrapolation : text
          extrapolation_method : text
          exclude_t0_from_interpolation : text
        * has_report : boolean
          report_pillar_dates : text
    }

    note right of ores_dq_yield_curve_configs_artefact_tbl
    The settings of one YieldCurve entry of a curveconfig.xml, one row per curve_definition in the YieldCurves section. The entry's identity is on the definition, its segments in curve_segment, its bootstrap settings in curve_bootstrap_config; this row holds the rest, each as the column its schema type calls for. Extrapolation and ExcludeT0FromInterpolation are ORE booleans, which accept Y, true and True among other spellings. They are held as the spelling the document used, so the export writes it back unchanged. The day counter is held the same way and refers to day_counter. - Artefact Table
    end note

    entity ores_dq_zero_conventions_artefact_tbl  #F7E5FF {
        --
        * dataset_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * tenant_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * id : text
        * version : integer
        * party_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * tenor_based : boolean
        * day_count_fraction : text
          compounding : text
          compounding_frequency : text
          tenor_calendar : text
          spot_lag : integer
          spot_calendar : text
          roll_convention : text
          end_of_month : boolean
          oresmd_uri : text
    }

    note right of ores_dq_zero_conventions_artefact_tbl
    Specifies the day count fraction and compounding method used when bootstrapping a discount or zero-rate curve in ORE. Corresponds to the <Zero> element in ORE conventions.xml. The id field is the natural key (ORE <Id> element). - Artefact Table
    end note

    entity ores_dq_zero_inflation_index_conventions_artefact_tbl  #F7E5FF {
        --
        * dataset_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * tenant_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * id : text
        * version : integer
        * party_id : uuid <<FK>>
        * region_name : text
        * region_code : text
        * revised : boolean
        * frequency : text
        * availability_lag : text
        * currency : text
          oresmd_uri : text
    }

    note right of ores_dq_zero_inflation_index_conventions_artefact_tbl
    Describes a zero-coupon inflation index: the region it is published for, how often it is published, how far behind its publication date it becomes available, and the currency it is quoted in. Corresponds to the <ZeroInflationIndex> element in ORE conventions.xml. The id field is the natural key (ORE <Id> element). Every field is required. The corpus sets all seven in all fifty-four elements, and ORE's schema holds them by value, so an element without one does not parse. The element carries one more field, RebasingEvents, and this table does not model it: the ORE type holds it as a list of doubles, and the refdata schema has no array column. No shipped file sets it. Rather than drop it silently the mapper counts every element that carries one in conventions_mapper::unmodelled under ZeroInflationIndex.RebasingEvents, so a document that uses it is excluded from the round-trip set and named in the measurement. - Artefact Table
    end note

